@@ -58,7 +58,7 @@ Every event has an audience: either all members or only specific users. Direct m
 | `state.json` | Accounts, session hashes, rooms, roles, the invite code, friendships, blocks, server settings. Written atomically by writing a temporary file and renaming it, and before each write the current valid version is kept as `state.json.bak`. Changes are collected for about 200 ms and reach the disk in a single write. For security relevant actions (password change and reset, sign out and session revocation, account deletion and kicking from the frequency, personal blocks and server bans, role changes, invite code renewal, changing the active group key) the success response is sent only after the file has been written and forced to disk (fsync), and requests waiting at the same time share one write. If the write fails, the request gets a 500 error, the change stays in memory and is written later by the retry loop. |
 | `messages/<id>.jsonl` | One encrypted message record per line for each room and direct message conversation. The last 20000 messages per room are kept. The total number of messages across all conversations is limited by `MAKS_TOPLAM_MESAJ` (500000 by default). When it is exceeded, the oldest messages of the conversations with the most messages are removed and a delete record is added to the log. A message line is queued for writing right away, the response does not wait for the write, and lines are not forced to disk with fsync. |
 | `server-icon/<hash>.bin` | The frequency photo (PNG, JPEG or WebP, not encrypted). The file name is the first 32 hex digits of the sha256 hash of the content. A new photo is first written to a temporary file, forced to disk and renamed, then its record (`serverIcon`) goes into `state.json` and the response is sent once that record is on disk, and the old file is deleted after that. At startup the file is read and its type and hash are checked, if they do not match the record is removed, and files that are not recorded are cleaned up. |
-| `uploads/<id>.bin` | Encrypted files and profile pictures. The total size is limited by `YUKLEME_KOTASI_MB` and the size per user by `KULLANICI_YUKLEME_KOTASI_MB`, and uploads not attached to a message are removed after one hour. |
+| `uploads/<id>.bin` | Encrypted files, profile pictures and cover photos. The total size is limited by `YUKLEME_KOTASI_MB` and the size per user by `KULLANICI_YUKLEME_KOTASI_MB`, and uploads not attached to a message are removed after one hour. |
 | `.kilit` | The lock of the running process. It prevents a second process from opening the same data folder. |
 
 If `state.json` is corrupt, the server tries to recover from `state.json.bak`. If it can, it first keeps the corrupt file as `state.json.bozuk-<time>` and logs what happened, and if it cannot, it refuses to start. Message bodies are opaque envelopes to the server and are never logged. Telsiz DJ states, presence, typing status and voice rosters exist only in memory and are reset when the server restarts.
@@ -85,6 +85,8 @@ Text room messages, profiles, identity bindings, voice signals and the Telsiz DJ
 
 Files and images are each encrypted with their own random key and nonce. The file key, name, type and dimensions travel inside the encrypted plaintext of the message, and the server sees only the size of the encrypted file. Photos other than GIFs are re-encoded with a canvas before upload, which removes metadata including the location.
 
+The profile picture and the cover photo are also uploads encrypted with their own keys. The profile picture is center-cropped to a square and re-encoded at 256x256, the cover is center-cropped to 3:1 and re-encoded as a 1200x400 JPEG. If the cover exceeds the same size limit as the profile picture (`avatarMaxBytes`), the qualities 0.85, 0.7 and 0.55 are tried in turn. The references to both images (`avatar` and `cover`: upload id, file key, nonce, type and dimensions) travel in the profile envelope encrypted with the group key. The server keeps only the upload ids (`avatarUploadId`, `coverUploadId`) in the user record and serves both images to everyone who is signed in. An upload can be linked only to the uploader's own profile and to a single field, cannot be attached to a message and is not removed by the sweep of unlinked uploads. A changed or removed image is deleted, and both are deleted when the account is deleted. A save from an older client that does not send the `coverUploadId` field removes the cover, so the envelope and the server record stay consistent. The client uses a reference from the envelope only if it matches the upload id on the server. Profile pictures are downloaded when the profile arrives, while the cover is downloaded only when it is about to be shown on the profile card or in the Settings preview.
+
 A new group key can be created. The new key becomes the active key and protects the content that follows, while older keys stay in the keyring on the device to read older messages. The active key only moves forward on a device: a key that a newer key replaced on that device is retired (`telsiz.keys.retired`) and the client never encrypts anything with it again. Only a newer key that the device holds in its keyring, and whose code really yields that key id, replaces the previous one. An unknown key id from the server retires nothing, so the server or an admin cannot retire the real key by announcing a made-up key id first. If the server or an admin switches the active key back to such an older key, sending stays off and a warning appears until a new key is created. So a key known to a removed member or an old invite link cannot be brought back for new content. The group key travels in the part of the invite link after the `#` sign, which browsers do not send to the server.
 
 ### Password and account
@@ -204,7 +206,7 @@ Third party actions in the workflows are pinned by commit SHA rather than by tag
 
 Telsiz is designed to hide content from the server, but it does not hide everything, and like any software it cannot be guaranteed to be free of vulnerabilities. The project has not had an independent security audit. The known limits are as follows.
 
-**Metadata the server sees.** The server cannot see message, file, profile or DJ content, but it does see: which user is online when and their status (including the invisible status), usernames, room names and the room list, roles, who writes to which room and direct message conversation, when and with what size, edit and delete events, the sizes of uploaded files, friendship and block relations, typing status (who is typing in which conversation and when), who is in which voice room, their mute and deafen state and whose camera is on, who calls whom in direct messages and when, whether a call is voice or video and how long it lasts, the timing and size of voice signals, which voice rooms have a Telsiz DJ state, the size of the DJ envelope and who updates it and when, session labels derived from the browser's user agent (for example the browser and operating system name), and the IP addresses of connecting devices. Display names, about me, custom status text and profile pictures are encrypted. The frequency name, the frequency introduction and the frequency photo are not encrypted and are public.
+**Metadata the server sees.** The server cannot see message, file, profile or DJ content, but it does see: which user is online when and their status (including the invisible status), usernames, room names and the room list, roles, who writes to which room and direct message conversation, when and with what size, edit and delete events, the sizes of uploaded files, friendship and block relations, typing status (who is typing in which conversation and when), who is in which voice room, their mute and deafen state and whose camera is on, who calls whom in direct messages and when, whether a call is voice or video and how long it lasts, the timing and size of voice signals, which voice rooms have a Telsiz DJ state, the size of the DJ envelope and who updates it and when, session labels derived from the browser's user agent (for example the browser and operating system name), and the IP addresses of connecting devices. Because the cover photo is downloaded only when a profile card is opened, the server can also see who opens whose card and when. Display names, about me, custom status text, profile pictures and cover photos are encrypted. The frequency name, the frequency introduction and the frequency photo are not encrypted and are public.
 
 **Code delivery in the web version.** In the web version the app code comes from the server on every visit. An active attacker who takes over the server, or an intermediary that terminates the HTTPS connection (a reverse proxy or a tunnel provider), could serve modified code and steal keys. This is the known limit of every end-to-end encrypted app that runs in a browser. The desktop app removes this risk because it carries the interface inside itself.
 
